 ## Limitations
 
-The historical table combines separate runs and workload versions. The selected s3 run has 20 identical recorded scalar losses and matched input, effective-batch, objective and seed checks. The selected s8 run has identical recorded scalar losses but incomplete input, effective-batch and objective pairing. Earlier s3 results remain in the history. s1 and s4 compare the first five backward-loss values against documented references, and s2 uses a tolerance-based comparison with incomplete workload pairing. An OOM baseline only shows the peak reached before failure; s1's candidate peak is reconstructed. Verification covers short training runs, not full training quality. Distributed training and compiled extensions are outside the current repair workflow.
+The historical table combines separate runs and workload versions. The selected s3 run has 20 identical recorded scalar losses and matched input, effective-batch, objective and seed checks. The selected s8 run has identical recorded scalar losses but incomplete input, effective-batch and objective pairing. Earlier s3 results remain in the history. s1 and s4 compare the first five backward-loss values against documented references, and s2 uses a tolerance-based comparison with incomplete workload pairing. An OOM baseline only shows the peak reached before failure; s1's selected CUDA peak is a recorded counter; its allocator-history reconstruction is incomplete. Verification covers short training runs, not full training quality. Distributed training and compiled extensions are outside the current repair workflow.
 
-Four scenarios have no recorded target success in this history. The latest October 30 evaluation met three of nine memory targets, below the target of six; matching a cause was often insufficient to produce a usable patch. The first-cause counts use the first saved diagnosis round, which can include correction requests within that round; raw category matches and validated diagnoses are separate measures. The two additional s2 precision-policy tests did not pass, and HF Trainer repair failed after a configuration edit passed its static check. They produced no successful memory-versus-speed comparison or new successful gallery cases.
+Four scenarios have no recorded target success in this history. The latest complete October 30 evaluation met three of nine memory targets, below the target of six; matching a cause was often insufficient to produce a usable patch. The first-cause counts use the first saved diagnosis round, which can include correction requests within that round; raw category matches and validated diagnoses are separate measures. The two additional s2 precision-policy tests did not pass, and HF Trainer repair failed after a configuration edit passed its static check. They produced no successful memory-versus-speed comparison or new successful gallery cases.
 
 The diagnosis comparison is one fixed-order pass without repeats; s5 and nanoGPT have unconfirmed reference causes and remain in its denominator. Two full-evidence Ultra replies were truncated. Long-input probes establish request handling, not diagnosis quality or the maximum context size. The saved-snapshot path now has real valid CLI and public-web diagnoses, but some model explanations still misstate category totals or inferred runtime settings. A large-file public upload timed out before analysis. Server-stored capture analysis is the default webpage flow; external-file upload is optional. Both use real Nebius inference and require the judge token on the public deployment. Preset live examples and the historical Verified runs gallery are available.
 
